% conclusion.tex — compressed for 8-page limit

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Topic shortcut confound.}
Three observations argue against a topic-level shortcut: (1) topic classification yields unstable OOD generalization (F1\,=\,0.541$\pm$0.207 on DD); (2) scrambled labels match correct labels (F1 gap $\leq$3.9\,pp); (3) MLM achieves strong detection without labels (F1\,$\geq$\,0.97 at $K \leq 5$). Domain exposure, not topic-level classification, drives detection.

\paragraph{Single-LLM confound.}
Both training and synthetic OOD data are generated by Qwen3-8B. ToxicChat~\citep{lin2023toxicchat} (204 human-written jailbreaks) provides partial resolution: all domain-adapted variants outperform vanilla by 18--42\,pp (Table~\ref{tab:human_data}). A cross-LLM experiment (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}) further isolates the confound: with Llama-3.1-8B jailbreaks and Qwen benign text, detection F1 recovers to 0.78--1.00; the residual ActorAttack shortfall (F1\,=\,0.80 vs.\ DD 1.00) reflects cross-LLM distributional differences rather than model failure.
Mixed-LLM training (250 Qwen + 250 Llama conversations) reveals a sharper finding: classification-based DAPT is the only robust adaptation across training compositions. On ToxicChat (zero-shot single-turn transfer), 9-class rises from F1\,=\,0.596 (Qwen-only) to [EXP16_TOXICCHAT_BALANCED] (mixed), while MLM drops to [EXP16_MLM_TOXICCHAT_BALANCED], below vanilla ([EXP16_VANILLA_TOXICCHAT_BALANCED]). Llama-only training confirms the pattern: MLM $\approx$ vanilla on all OOD sets (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}). Domain exposure closes the OOD gap only when training data shares the target LLM's distributional signature; classification supervision generalizes across LLM sources.

\paragraph{Variance reversal on human data.}
On synthetic OOD, classification supervision reduces cross-seed variance 4--20$\times$ over MLM; on ToxicChat, this reverses (9-class std\,=\,0.087 vs.\ JB-MLM 0.013). Classification stabilizes within-distribution predictions; MLM's cross-distribution advantage on human data is specific to single-source training conditions.

\paragraph{Encoder capacity confound.}
DeBERTa-v3-base (184M), RoBERTa-base (125M), and RoBERTa-large (355M) span a 2.8$\times$ capacity range but also differ in pretraining objectives (RTD vs.\ MLM) and tokenization. The capacity-dependent pattern across three models (Section~\ref{sec:cross_encoder}) is suggestive but not conclusive: confirming that model size rather than architecture drives the pattern requires same-architecture scaling (e.g., DeBERTa-base vs.\ DeBERTa-large) with matched pretraining.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Our ablation across three encoder scales, three OOD attack families, and two training compositions yields three findings. First, for base-size encoders (125--184M), classification-based DAPT is the only adaptation that robustly bridges the OOD gap across all conditions; unsupervised pretraining closes $\sim$60\,pp on single-source data but collapses on mixed-LLM training. For the largest encoder (355M), pretrained representations already suffice on structurally complex attacks. Second, as model size increases, the set of attacks requiring domain adaptation narrows. Third, classification supervision stabilizes within-distribution predictions (variance reduced 4--20$\times$) but this benefit reverses on human data. Mixed-LLM training with classification-based DAPT achieves the strongest single-turn human-data transfer (ToxicChat F1\,=\,[EXP16_TOXICCHAT_BALANCED]); multi-turn human evaluation remains future work.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Dataset scale and diversity.}
The training set uses synthetic conversations generated by Qwen3-8B. We validate robustness to dataset scale by doubling the training corpus from 500 to 1{,}000 conversations: classification-based DAPT maintains strong OOD generalization (9-class DD F1\,=\,0.889$\pm$0.041, ActorAttack\,=\,0.913$\pm$0.041), and the hierarchy 9-class $\gg$ MLM $\approx$ vanilla holds (DAPT advantage 40--56\,pp over vanilla). The perturbation evaluation ($n$=76) remains underpowered; tier boundaries in Table~\ref{tab:perturbation} correspond to 1--3 misclassifications. All multi-turn data is synthetic. ToxicChat (human-authored, single-turn) confirms transfer across data sources (+18--42\,pp over vanilla; Section~\ref{sec:human_data}), but whether the full evidence hierarchy generalizes to human-generated multi-turn attacks is open.
Cross-LLM validation with Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct as the target model reveals that jailbreak detection transfers across LLMs (F1 recovers to 0.80--1.00 when the benign distribution is held constant), but the benign baseline is LLM-specific: replacing Qwen benign text with Llama benign text causes false-positive rates to spike (F1 drops to 0.37--0.43). A per-class error decomposition (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}, Table~\ref{tab:cross_llm_error}) confirms that true positive rates are identical across benign conditions; the F1 collapse is driven entirely by false positives on unfamiliar benign text. The residual cross-LLM degradation on structurally complex attacks (ActorAttack F1 = 0.80 vs.\ 0.997 in-distribution) indicates that some jailbreak features remain source-specific. Mixed-LLM training partially resolves this gap: classification-based DAPT on Qwen+Llama data achieves ToxicChat F1\,=\,[EXP16_TOXICCHAT_BALANCED] (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}), though multi-turn human evaluation remains open.

\paragraph{Lexical separability.}
TF-IDF logistic regression achieves F1 = 0.961 on OOD Deceptive Delight with near-perfect per-prefix performance; this indicates substantial lexical separability on this attack family. On DD alone, we cannot distinguish whether continued pretraining captures beyond-lexical features or exploits the same lexical cues. ActorAttack resolves this question: TF-IDF collapses to F1 = 0.261 while domain-adapted models maintain F1 $\geq$ 0.89, a $\geq$63\,pp gap that demonstrates continued pretraining captures features beyond lexical overlap on structurally complex attacks. Whether this beyond-lexical advantage extends to other structurally complex attack families beyond ActorAttack requires further evaluation.

\paragraph{Strategy annotation quality.}
Persuasion strategy labels are produced by Qwen3-8B with structured prompting. Inter-annotator agreement with a human sample ($n$=50) yields Cohen's $\kappa$=0.78, which indicates substantial but imperfect agreement. The small human validation sample and reliance on a single annotator model limit confidence in label quality. We plan to release the annotation prompts and data to enable independent verification.

\paragraph{OOD strategy prediction.}
While the 9-class model achieves high detection F1 on OOD attacks (0.997$\pm$0.005 on Deceptive Delight), its strategy-level predictions do not generalize: OOD strategy accuracy is 30.7\% ($\kappa$=0.126). The interpretability provided by the persuasion taxonomy is currently limited to known attack families where strategy labels are reliable.

\paragraph{FITD discriminative power.}
FITD provides minimal discriminative signal: both vanilla and 9-class achieve F1 $\geq$ 0.990 at all prefixes. FITD serves primarily as a confirmation that simple OOD attacks do not challenge any variant, rather than as a meaningful evaluation axis.

\paragraph{Scope.}
Only persuasion-mediated multi-turn attacks fall within scope. Single-turn token-level attacks~\citep{zou2023gcg}, non-persuasive multi-turn strategies, and attacks targeting modalities other than text are not addressed. The user-only input assumption (excluding assistant responses) may miss information in attacks where the model's own responses contribute to the escalation trajectory.

\paragraph{Single-turn transfer.}
ToxicChat jailbreaks are exclusively single-turn, while all training data consists of multi-turn conversations (5--14 turns). ToxicChat performance therefore reflects zero-shot single-turn transfer, not standard multi-turn OOD evaluation. The temporal aggregator can learn sequence length as a spurious feature: doubling the training corpus from 500 to 1{,}000 conversations drops 9-class ToxicChat macro F1 from 0.596 to [EXP17_TOXICCHAT_BALANCED], while multi-turn OOD detection remains stable (DD\,=\,0.889, ActorAttack\,=\,0.913). This length-dependent fragility confirms that the architecture targets multi-turn detection; single-turn robustness requires architectural changes beyond this work's scope.

\paragraph{Computation cost.}
Stage~1 continued pretraining requires approximately 30 minutes on a single A100 GPU per variant. Stage~2 training completes in under 5 minutes. Total pipeline cost for all seven variants is under 4 GPU-hours, making the approach accessible for replication.

